mptcp-bpf-red (1.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Retransmit like the default scheduler: call mptcp_set_timeout() on every
    pick and add a get_retrans that prefers a subflow with nothing
    outstanding at TCP level, and a backup one only when no subflow makes
    progress. Without them the MPTCP retransmission timer stayed at
    TCP_RTO_MIN (200 ms) whatever the paths' round-trip times, and each time
    it fired get_send sent the oldest unacknowledged data again, usually on
    the path already holding it (measured on two shaped 8 Mbit/s paths:
    goodput 2.8 -> 10.5 Mbit/s).

 -- Yannick Chabanois <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 13:14:19 +0000

mptcp-bpf-red (1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Schedule only subflows that can actually carry data: skip ones the path
    manager marked stale, ones that have not finished joining, and ones whose
    send buffer is already full because the path is blackholed. Marking every
    subflow fed the dead path a dfrag on every push round, which TCP then
    retransmitted while MPTCP reinjected the same data elsewhere. On a
    three-WAN bench with one WAN blackholed mid-transfer this cut traffic from
    ~19x the payload to ~2.5x and removed a 5 s application-visible stall,
    while keeping the redundancy the scheduler exists for.

 -- Yannick Chabanois <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000

